From 21767c22070568bb21fcd787c7214debccbd0c58 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 06:34:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10273/11362] Correct the claim enforcement: it never fired,
 and the deadlock was still live

An adversarial review of yesterday's REQ-INFRA-6820 commit found its central
claim false in the deployed state. Both halves confirmed by reproduction before
touching anything.

THE FEATURE WAS INERT. `check()` identifies the committing session solely by
`CARNOT_AGENT_SCOPE_ID`. A repo-wide grep found exactly one reference to that
variable: its own definition. Nothing exported it -- not the conductor, not
`declare()`'s output, not the docstring -- so every committer read as
unidentified and cross-session enforcement never fired for anyone. The earlier
commit appeared to work only because I exported the variable by hand while
testing it.

THE DEADLOCK WAS STILL LIVE. The older out-of-scope branch read
`committer is None or run_id == committer`, so an unattributable commit was
judged by EVERY declaration. Reproduced exactly, following the tool's own
documented usage: agent A declares a.py, agent B declares b.py, B stages only
its own b.py, refused by A's record. The comment in `check()` claimed
per-session judging had removed this; the None fall-through kept it, and
`test_two_scopes_are_both_enforced` locked it in as a requirement. That test now
asserts the corrected policy and explains what it used to demand.

So the two policies were paired the worst possible way: maximal freeze, zero
enforcement. Corrections:

- `declare()` prints `export CARNOT_AGENT_SCOPE_ID=<id>`.
- An unattributable commit is judged only when attribution is unambiguous --
  exactly one live declaration. Honest cost: with several sessions and no
  exported id, out-of-scope checking is off, which the export line mitigates.
- Staleness now applies to the out-of-scope branch. It did not, so the refusal
  text promising that a claim older than 4h "stops blocking on its own" was
  false for the branch that actually fired -- a crashed agent locked everyone
  out permanently.

TIMESTAMPS: ONE PARSE, ONE CLOCK. Ordering keyed on the raw string while expiry
parsed, so they disagreed three ways. `"!bad-timestamp"` was unparseable, hence
never stale, while `"!"` sorts below every ISO stamp -- one corrupt file owned
its patterns forever, under a comment claiming an unusable timestamp loses every
comparison. `"10:00:00Z"` vs `"10:00:00.500000+00:00"`: `.` < `Z`, so the later
stamp sorted older. `"08:00:00-05:00"` is 13:00Z and sorted before `"09:00:00Z"`.
Only the first is reachable while `declare()` is the sole writer, and a
comparison right only under that monopoly is not right.

`*.py` CLAIMED THE WHOLE REPOSITORY. fnmatch's `*` crosses `/`, so a one-line
`--scope '*.py'` owned every Python file in the tree against every other session
for four hours. A wildcard-leading pattern with no directory separator is now
advisory, like `*`. `scripts/*.py` stays enforceable.

GUARD 1: EQUALITY, NOT CONTAINMENT. `is_relative_to` passed a package at
`<root>/.claude/worktrees/agent-abc/python/carnot` -- the real layout here, so a
leaked worktree PYTHONPATH in a main-checkout run went undetected -- and nested
repo clones, which this repo demonstrably grows. It now requires exactly
`<tests_root>/python/carnot`. `tests/integration/conftest.py` was missing the
guard entirely although `testpaths` covers it and `test_install.py` imports
carnot.

TESTS THAT NOW BITE. The review proved five mutations left the suite green:
`check()`'s enforcement call replaced by `{}`, `declare()`'s warning call
replaced by `{}`, the age key destroyed (the run ids happened to sort with age,
so the ordering property was decorative), the universal charset shrunk to `"*"`,
and the conftest call replaced by `pass`. Every rule was tested through a pure
function and no rule was tested through the entry point production uses -- the
exp5879 hollow-fix shape again. All five are RED now, via tests that build a
real git repo and call `check()` and `declare()`. Also added: an autouse fixture
clearing the ambient scope id, because three shipped tests failed when the
variable was exported, which is the state any agent using this tool is in.

Declaration-time warning also missed glob-vs-glob overlap, including two
sessions declaring the identical glob -- the likeliest collision of all.

95 tests green across the four affected files. Live check verified both ways:
unidentified (how the conductor commits) exits 0, so nothing is frozen;
identified refuses correctly. Conductor still active throughout.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw

Two further catches while landing this, both by the project's own guards:
`qa-layer-audit-scope-check` refused until `worktree_import_guard.py` was
classified (it is now a GUARD_TARGET, with the reason that a silent non-firing
there turns every proof green against a file that was never loaded), and three
tests in `test_harness_integrity_lint.py` failed because my own shell had the
scope id exported -- the same ambient-environment sensitivity the review found,
in a second file. Both fixed here.
